                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2909140,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "They had wells for fresh water. Many of their houses were built around a courtyard and were constructed of stone. The village contained an impressive monument, seven half ton stones standing in a semicircle around a spring that might have been used for ceremonial purposes. Atlit Yam may have been destroyed swiftly by a tsunami, or climate change may have caused glaciers to melt and sea levels to rise, flooding the village gradually. Whatever the cause, it now lies 10 meters below the surface of the Mediterranean, buried under sand at the bottom of the sea.",
            "children": [
